The hidden cursor stays hidden after another app was in front and at the Dock's edge, and is set without being undone.

This repository's follow-up to patch 0013 (Wine merge request 11880 v2, Elvin Hayatov), to be offered on that merge
request (version 2, replacing version 1 of r7 to r9). Three changes to -[WineApplicationController updateCursor:]
and -applicationDidBecomeActive: in the Mac driver:

- The presentation options (Cursor Location Assistance off while a hidden or custom cursor is active) are assigned
  only when they change, and before the cursor is set, not after.
- The hidden cursor is set again after anything that may have replaced it: on activation, again 250 ms later, and
  at the first mouse move over a Wine window after activation or after the pointer was over another app's window.
  The driver keeps two transparent cursors and alternates between them, because AppKit keeps reporting the one it
  last set as current while macOS shows its own arrow, so setting that one again may change nothing.
- While the game wants the cursor hidden and Wine is the active app, a mouse move over a Wine window sets the hidden
  cursor again (the other one of the two) once 100 ms have passed since it was last set, so at most ten times a
  second and only while the mouse moves.

Why: with 0013 alone, a game that keeps its cursor hidden across a Cmd-Tab (its show count stays below zero, so Wine
sends the driver no new SetCursor; Skyrim SE does this) showed the Mac arrow over the game after the switch back,
with and without mouse movement. 0013's only re-apply, at activation, did not hold, and later mouse moves never
re-set the cursor, because +[NSCursor currentCursor] still named the transparent one.

Lab evidence (mavrik S211, M1 Pro, macOS 27.0.1; a Windows probe hiding the cursor two ways: ShowCursor(FALSE)
with a full-screen ClipCursor, and an exclusive foreground DirectInput8 mouse; the steps posted as mouse and key
events; the cursor read from 64x64 screen captures with and without -C; only readings where the game and the driver
both asked for a hidden cursor count; .factory/design-passes/2026-10-23-s211/cursor.md in mavrik):
- Block 1 (2 runs per cell): r6 showed the cursor after Cmd-Tab, the Dock edge and a shake in both modes; 0013
  failed Cmd-Tab in both modes and the Dock edge, the shake and the baseline once each in DirectInput mode; with
  this patch's first change only, everything passed except Cmd-Tab (2/2 failed in both modes).
- Block 3 (Cmd-Tab only, 3 runs, DirectInput): r6 3/3 and the first change alone 3/3 showed the cursor; with both
  changes 0/3, both 1.5 s after the return with no movement and after movement.
- Block 4 (3 runs per cell, all steps, both modes): r6 showed it 3/3 after every trigger; with 0013 and this patch,
  0/3 everywhere.
Open: before the first mouse movement after a game hides its cursor, the arrow can still show (DirectInput mode, 2 of
3 runs in blocks 3 and 4; never with r6, whose [NSCursor hide] needs no movement). Not explained yet.

Why version 2 (mavrik S216, macOS 27.0.1): over a full-screen game, the Mac arrow appeared as soon as the pointer
touched the bottom edge of the screen and stayed until Wine lost and regained focus (the probe in DirectInput mode,
and ABZU). A sampler of the displayed system cursor every 20 ms showed the cursor seed change once and the arrow
appear at y 964 to 980 of 982, over Wine's window, with the Dock's full-display window (layer 20) under it, while the
probe stayed in front with DirectInput acquired and Wine received every mouse event. No SetCursor reached the driver,
and the check against +[NSCursor currentCursor] never re-set the cursor in a minute of movement, so AppKit still named
the transparent one and version 1 had nothing to react to. SDL's fix for a similar fault (SDL 2.30.2, libsdl-org/SDL
pull request 9294: re-apply the cursor when +[NSCursor currentCursor] is not the wanted one) would miss it the same
way. Read from AppKit's code (macOS 27): -[NSCursor set] skips a custom cursor that is the one it last set, which is
why each re-apply sets the other transparent cursor.

